# config/subsys/Kconfig.packages — Signed application packages
#
# crates/core/pkg is the format, the verification and the admission; the
# installer service that runs them is userspace/services/pkgd (USERSPACE_PKGD,
# generated into config/Kconfig.userspace). The device policy that says which
# author keys are admitted is not here: it is the `[signer.NAME]` sections of
# the signed topology (TOPOLOGY_MAX_SIGNERS in config/subsys/Kconfig.topology).

config PKG_MAX_KB
    int "Largest package the installer accepts (KiB)"
    range 16 65536
    default 4096 if HWCLASS_SERVER
    default 256 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 1024
    help
      The installer (PKGD.ELF) reads a package whole into one buffer before it
      verifies a byte of it: the signature covers the metadata, the images are
      checked against the hashes that metadata carries, and the rows are
      parsed only after both. A package larger than this is refused unread.
      The ceiling is also the installer's biggest static buffer.

      Cost: PKG_MAX_KB of RAM in PKGD.ELF (.bss), either ISA, and the same as
      the ceiling of PKG.ELF's frame budget (the tool holds the file it is told
      to install whole, and a fetched update bundle (`pkg fetch-update`)
      arrives in the same buffer). The kernel's boot-time apply reads one
      staged package at a time into the heap, at most this, and frees it.

config PKG_META_MAX_KB
    int "Largest manifest or warrant section of a package (KiB)"
    range 1 64
    default 16
    help
      A package carries two small texts besides its images: the metadata
      (name, hardware classes, ABI and interface levels, one hash per image)
      and the rows (the `[task.NAME]` sections that become part of the
      topology). Each is at most this. A larger one is refused before the
      signature is checked.

      Cost: none by itself.

config PKG_MAX_IMAGES
    int "Programs one package may carry"
    range 1 64
    default 8
    help
      Every image of a package is a program with exactly one row, so this is
      also the most rows one package adds to the topology. The metadata parser
      keeps a table of this many images on its stack.

      Cost: 56 B of stack per entry while a package is read, either ISA.

config PKG_MAX_STAGED
    int "Packages staged at once"
    range 1 256
    default 4 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 64 if HWCLASS_SERVER
    default 16
    help
      The installer stages a package as one file on the package volume (the
      native volume mounted at /pkg) and judges a new
      one against the topology AND the packages already staged (their rows are
      merged first), so it keeps the rows text of each staged package in an
      arena of this many slots of PKG_META_MAX_KB. A package staged beyond
      this is refused (`no room`); remove one first.

      Cost: PKG_MAX_STAGED x PKG_META_MAX_KB of RAM in PKGD.ELF (.bss), either
      ISA. The kernel keeps the rows text of each package it applies for the
      life of the boot (the topology borrows it): at most the same product of
      heap.

config PKG_SNAPSHOTS_KEPT
    int "Restore points of the package volume kept"
    range 1 16
    default 3
    help
      Before the kernel writes a new package's programs at boot it snapshots
      the package volume; a boot that is confirmed good keeps the snapshot as a
      restore point. The installer (PKGD.ELF) keeps this many of the newest and
      asks the kernel to delete the older ones at the next boot, so the volume
      does not fill with blocks that only an old snapshot names. The snapshot
      of a boot that is still on probation is never deleted. The kernel stops
      applying new packages while the volume holds 32 snapshots (the table's
      size), so an installer that does not prune is noticed.

      Cost: the blocks the snapshots retain (what the volume changed since
      each), either ISA; nothing in RAM.

config PKG_MAX_API
    int "Interface levels one package may require"
    range 1 32
    default 8
    help
      A package states the lowest level it needs of each IDL interface it
      calls (`[api]`). The device refuses it when an interface is missing or
      older. More entries than this are refused by the metadata parser.

      Cost: 24 B of stack per entry while a package is read, either ISA.

config PKG_FETCH_TFTP
    bool "Installer may fetch a package from a TFTP server (development builds)"
    depends on BUILD_TYPE_DEV && USERSPACE_PKGD
    default y
    help
      `pkg fetch` gets a package, and `pkg fetch-update` a whole-system update
      bundle, from the source the rows in /fat/PKG/SOURCE.CFG describe: a
      pinned HTTPS server. With this on, the
      same file may instead name a TFTP server on the development host
      (`tftp=10.0.2.2`, `file=NAME.PKG`: QEMU's `-netdev user,tftp=DIR`
      gateway, or tools/dev/tftp_serve.py), so a package built on the host goes to
      the device without a disk cycle or a TLS server.

      TFTP carries no authentication and the installer asks for none: it only
      delivers bytes, and the package is installed because its own signature
      verifies against the signers of the device's signed topology. A package
      fetched this way has no more authority than one on the disk, and a forged
      one is refused and journalled like any other.

      Development builds only (BUILD_TYPE): a release or certifiable .config
      cannot set it, and PKGD.ELF refuses to compile with it on otherwise.
      Off, a `tftp=` row is a source that is not one and is refused.

      Cost: about 2 KiB of code in PKGD.ELF and no RAM beyond the UDP socket
      the image's seccomp profile already grants; the same on every ISA.
